 conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, grumpy, uncooperative, weak memory for numbers.\n\nC: makes correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology, difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education.\n\nD: responds adequately to unexpected events, concentrates very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as arrogant, relatively weak leadership, hot temper, know-it-all.\n\nWe have discussed B's communication issues, C not yet discussed, A's strengths, D's strengths and concerns.\n\nNow as Agent 2, we need to make a focused argumentative contribution, referencing prior discussion. Perhaps we can bring in Candidate C now, as they haven't been discussed yet, to ensure coverage. We can argue that C's reliability and decision quality are strong, but communication issues are serious. Or we could argue that D's arrogance and weak leadership are more severe than A's unorganized nature. Or we could shift to C if we think his strengths outweigh concerns.\n\nWe need to decide our vote. The panel hasn't covered C yet, but we need to cover all candidates before final. However, we can still give a provisional vote now. Perhaps we can say we still favor D, but we need to discuss C before final. Or we could shift to A. Let's weigh.\n\nD: strong problem solving, concentration, responsibility, handling unexpected events. Concerns: arrogance, weak leadership, hot temper, know-it-all. Those could affect crew cooperation severely. In long-haul, crew cooperation is crucial. A's leadership is strong, but unorganized and not good at criticism. Unorganized could affect attention and procedural compliance. Arrogance could be more disruptive than being unorganized? Hard to say.\n\nC: reliability 100%, correct decisions quickly, calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. Concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education. Communication issues could affect crew cooperation. Egocentric may be similar to arrogance. Not willing to further education may affect adaptability.\n\nB: strong stress handling, conscientious, good weather assessment, computer skills, but grumpy and uncooperative, weak memory for numbers.\n\nGiven the criteria, we need a candidate with strong reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nLet's score roughly:\n\nA: Decision quality (anticipate dangerous) strong, technical (complex connections, spatial vision) strong, leadership strong, but attention (unorganized) weak, adaptability (not good at criticism) weak, communication unknown, crew cooperation maybe good due to leadership but could be impacted by show-off perception.\n\nB: Reliability (conscientious) strong, stress resilience strong, technical (weather, computer) strong, but crew cooperation poor (grumpy, uncooperative), attention (weak memory for numbers) weak, communication poor.\n\nC: Reliability 100% strong, decision quality strong, stress resilience (calm in crisis) strong, technical (understands technology) strong, but crew cooperation poor (communication difficulty, egocentric), adaptability low (not willing to further education).\n\nD: Reliability unknown but responsibility serious, stress resilience adequate, technical (problem solving) strong, decision quality strong, attention strong (concentrates), crew cooperation concerns (arrogance, weak leadership, hot temper), adaptability unknown.\n\nThus each has a major crew cooperation issue: A maybe less, D has arrogance, C has egocentric, B has uncooperative. Which is most severe? Hard.\n\nWe could argue that A's leadership and safety anticipation outweigh his organization issues, making him best. Or D's problem solving and responsibility outweigh his arrogance.\n\nGiven we need to cover C, we could bring C into discussion, but we also need to give a vote. Perhaps we can keep D as provisional but note we need to discuss C. Or we could shift to A. Let's see the panel's current state: Agent 1 supports D, Agent 3 supports A, Agent 2 (us) previously supported D. Now after Agent 1's defense of D, we could either maintain D or shift. To move towards convergence, we need to consider all candidates. Perhaps we can propose that we still favor D but we need to discuss C before final. That would be a focused contribution: \"I still recommend D, but we haven't examined C's reliability and decision quality, which could be decisive.\" Provide evidence: C's reliability 100%, correct decisions quickly, calm in crisis, understands technology. Concern: communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education. Compare to D's concerns. That adds new candidate coverage.\n\nThus we can keep vote D, but bring C into discussion. That satisfies candidate coverage rule.\n\nThus produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE: \"I remain with D, but we need to discuss Candidate C's strengths (reliability, decision quality, calmness, technical understanding) and concerns (communication, egocentric, lack of further education) to ensure we have covered all candidates before final.\" Provide vote D.\n\nNow produce METADATA_JSON with vote \"Candidate D\". Ensure format.\n\nProceed."}
